### Marching Squares Algorithm

I have organised my project so that in the main function I only have a loop
which reads all the commands one by one, executing all commands.

I organised my files such as:

- marching_squares.c: main loop
- march_squares_functions.c/.h: functions that implement the commands that work with
  pixel and grid arrays
- change_pixel.c/.h: functions that change the pixel array
- init_contour.c/.h: functions that print the contour

For the implementation of this algorithm I have used a three dimensional dinamically
allocated array named suggestively - pixel.

In main function I have divided the code into 7 sections for each command:

# READ section

In the READ section I have implemented the read function. Before entering the
read function I have freed the previously read images and previous grid patterns
to avoid memory leaks and overwriting of the values stored.

# WRITE section

In the WRITE section I have implemented the write function that displays all
information about the image.

# RESIZE section

In the RESIZE section I have implemented the resize function that enlarges the pixel
array by a factor of 4, updating its dimensions and copying the pixel data into a new
resized array.
Firstly, I allocate memory to a new three dimensional array named pixel_resize that
has the values of height and width each multiplied by 4.
Then, I copy the data from the original pixel array to the pixel_resize array by iterating
through the new values of height and width and calculating the position of the original
pixel (orig_i = i / 4, orig_j = j / 4) and copying its color data across the resized array.
At the end, the pixel array is freed and the funtion returns the new pixel_resize array.

# GRID section

In the GRID section I have implemented the generate_grid function that generates
the grid for a particular image. The grid is a two dimensional matrix that contains
only values of 0 and 1. At the end of the section I print the grid array.
Similarly to the read function I have freed the previously used grid array.
Firstly, I dinamically allocate memory to a two dimensional array named grid that
stores (height / 4 + 1) X (width / 4 + 1) elements.
Then, I calculate the values of the grid array based on the values in the pixel array
by calculating the average of the sum of the current pixel element and his neighbors
in an X shape.
Afterwards, I convert the grid to values of 0 and 1 based on the threshold value = 200
and return grid.

# MARCH section

In the MARCH section I have implemented the march function that applies the marching squares
algorithm.
Firstly, I check if the GRID command was run beforehand.
Afterwards, I apply the generate_grid function to generate the grid array.
Based on the values in the grid array, I create the march_grid array that stores values
only from 0 to 15.
Then, I modify the pixel array based on the 16 cases of the marching squares algorithm.

# INIT_CONTUR section

In the INIT_CONTUR section I have implemented the init_contur function that
prints all 16 cases of the marching squares algorithm.

# EXIT section

In the EXIT section I make sure I don't have any memory leaks then print the
final and most important message "Gigel a terminat".
